{% extends 'insar/insar.xml.j2' %}
{% block idPurp %}
This is the phi look vector map for a pair of Sentinel-1 {{ granule_type }} granules, processed using GAMMA software. The pixel values indicate the SAR look vector orientation angle at each map pixel, ranging from -pi to pi, with east being 0, north being pi/2, and south being -pi/2. It is a 32-bit float GeoTIFF projected to {{ projection }}, and has a pixel spacing of {{ pixel_spacing|int }} m.
{% endblock %}
{% block idAbs %}
&lt;DIV STYLE="text-align:Left;"&gt;&lt;DIV&gt;&lt;DIV&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;This is the phi look vector map for a pair of Sentinel-1 {{ granule_type }} granules, processed using GAMMA software. The look vector orientation angle is defined as the angle between the East direction and the projection of the look vector (pointing back towards the sensor) on the horizontal surface plane. The pixel values indicate the SAR look vector orientation angle at each map pixel, with values of 0 indicating east, pi/2 indicating north, and -pi/2 indicating south. The range of the orientation angle is -pi to pi, expressed in radians. It is a 32-bit float GeoTIFF projected to {{ projection }}, and has a pixel spacing of {{ pixel_spacing|int }} m.&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;Reference Scene: {{ reference_granule_name }}&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;Secondary Scene: {{ secondary_granule_name }}&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;Processing Date/Time: {{ processing_date.isoformat(timespec='seconds') }}&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;An important use of look vectors expressed in map geometry is the interpretation of 3D surface deformation. The main application is monitoring landslides and glaciers, where there is the assumption of motion along the gradient of the topographic height. Differential interferometry permits the deformation component in the SAR look direction to be estimated using the displacement component in the look direction and the direction angles (elevation theta and orientation phi) in the map geometry. The GAMMA program dispmap_vec can be used to perform these estimations.&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;Refer to ASF's &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;A href="https://hyp3-docs.asf.alaska.edu/guides/insar_product_guide/" target="_blank" &gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;InSAR Product Guide&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/A&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt; for more information about On Demand InSAR products.&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;The Sentinel-1 mission is designed to have two satellites in orbit, each with a 12-day repeat cycle. The satellites use the same orbit pattern, but are offset to allow for the possibility of repeat imagery every 6 days. To learn more about the mission observation scenario and the four Sentinel-1 satellites that have contributed to the mission since the launch of Sentinel-1A on April 3, 2014, refer to &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;A href="https://hyp3-docs.asf.alaska.edu/sentinel1/" target="_blank" &gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;https://hyp3-docs.asf.alaska.edu/sentinel1&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/A&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;.&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;More information about the Sentinel-1 mission is available at &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;A href="https://sentiwiki.copernicus.eu/web/s1-mission" target="_blank" &gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;https://sentiwiki.copernicus.eu/web/s1-mission&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/A&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;.&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;Additional information about Sentinel-1 data, imagery, tools and applications is available at &lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;A href="https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/data/platforms/space-based-platforms/sentinel-1" target="_blank" &gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;https://www.earthdata.nasa.gov/data/platforms/space-based-platforms/sentinel-1&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/A&gt;&lt;SPAN&gt;.&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;
{% endblock %}
{% block dataLineageStatement %}
The phi look vector map is generated from the SLC parameter file using GAMMA's look_vector program. Refer to the product readme file for details on the InSAR processing workflow.
{% endblock %}
